{"module":"borders","voice":"female","duration":218.1,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Borders: who gets to be American","start":0.0,"end":2.1,"words":[[0.07,0.49],[0.74,0.88],[0.88,1.16],[1.16,1.3],[1.3,1.44],[1.44,2.0]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Immigration law has always been a way of deciding who counts as American.","start":2.9,"end":6.98,"words":[[2.95,3.57],[3.57,3.7],[3.7,3.88],[3.88,4.26],[4.26,4.45],[4.45,4.57],[4.57,4.76],[4.76,4.88],[4.88,5.38],[5.38,5.5],[5.5,5.88],[5.88,6.0],[6.0,6.56]]},{"el":"intro","text":"For most of U.S. history, it did so openly by race.","start":7.33,"end":11.38,"words":[[7.37,7.5],[7.5,7.83],[7.83,7.96],[7.96,8.3],[8.63,9.16],[9.39,9.52],[9.52,9.72],[9.72,9.92],[9.92,10.39],[10.39,10.58],[10.58,10.92]]},{"el":"h0","text":"“Free white persons”","start":11.73,"end":13.24,"words":[[11.76,12.06],[12.06,12.47],[12.47,13.09]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The first Naturalization Act (1790) limited citizenship by naturalization to “free white persons.”","start":13.94,"end":21.11,"words":[[13.99,14.13],[14.13,14.41],[14.41,15.53],[15.53,15.74],[15.74,16.73],[16.98,17.47],[17.47,18.17],[18.17,18.38],[18.38,19.51],[19.51,19.65],[19.65,19.86],[19.86,20.14],[20.14,20.63]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Courts then had to decide who was white.","start":21.46,"end":23.7,"words":[[21.51,21.76],[21.76,21.95],[21.95,22.13],[22.13,22.26],[22.26,22.63],[22.63,22.75],[22.75,22.94],[22.94,23.25]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In Ozawa (1922) the Supreme Court ruled a Japanese immigrant was not; in Thind (1923) it ruled that an Indian Sikh immigrant was not either, even though anthropologists classed him as “Caucasian.”","start":24.05,"end":38.36,"words":[[24.11,24.27],[24.27,24.65],[24.65,25.79],[26.06,26.21],[26.21,26.67],[26.67,26.9],[26.9,27.2],[27.2,27.36],[27.36,27.97],[27.97,28.58],[28.58,28.81],[28.81,29.11],[29.38,29.53],[29.53,29.91],[29.91,31.13],[31.4,31.55],[31.55,31.86],[31.86,32.09],[32.09,32.24],[32.24,32.7],[32.7,32.93],[32.93,33.54],[33.54,33.77],[33.77,34.0],[34.0,34.38],[34.64,34.95],[34.95,35.18],[35.18,36.32],[36.32,36.7],[36.7,36.93],[36.93,37.09],[37.09,37.85]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Racial bars to naturalization weren't fully removed until 1952.","start":38.71,"end":44.4,"words":[[38.76,39.26],[39.26,39.51],[39.51,39.67],[39.67,41.01],[41.01,41.34],[41.34,41.68],[41.68,42.18],[42.18,42.6],[42.6,43.85]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Chinese Exclusion","start":45.3,"end":46.85,"words":[[45.35,45.94],[45.94,46.7]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The Page Act (1875) effectively barred Chinese women, and the Chinese Exclusion Act (1882) barred Chinese laborers, the first U.S. law to exclude a group by nationality.","start":47.55,"end":60.71,"words":[[47.6,47.75],[47.75,48.13],[48.13,48.36],[48.36,49.57],[49.84,50.52],[50.52,50.75],[50.75,51.28],[51.28,51.73],[52.0,52.22],[52.22,52.38],[52.38,52.91],[52.91,53.59],[53.59,53.82],[53.82,54.65],[54.92,55.14],[55.14,55.67],[55.67,56.36],[56.62,56.77],[56.77,57.08],[57.08,57.45],[57.83,57.98],[57.98,58.14],[58.14,58.67],[58.67,58.82],[58.82,59.12],[59.12,59.35],[59.35,60.18]]},{"el":"s1","text":"It wasn't repealed until 1943.","start":61.06,"end":63.96,"words":[[61.11,61.25],[61.25,61.67],[61.67,62.09],[62.09,62.45],[62.45,63.51]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Chinese immigrants were held and interrogated at Angel Island in San Francisco Bay (1910–1940).","start":64.31,"end":71.69,"words":[[64.36,64.87],[64.87,65.52],[65.52,65.67],[65.67,65.96],[65.96,66.17],[66.17,67.05],[67.05,67.19],[67.19,67.7],[67.7,68.14],[68.14,68.28],[68.28,68.5],[68.5,69.23],[69.23,69.44],[69.44,71.19]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Quotas by race","start":72.59,"end":74.07,"words":[[72.64,73.26],[73.26,73.53],[73.53,73.88]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Immigration Act of 1924 set quotas based on the national origins of the U.S. population in 1890, to favor Northern Europeans, and barred almost all immigration from Asia.","start":74.77,"end":87.72,"words":[[74.82,74.96],[74.96,75.69],[75.69,75.91],[75.91,76.06],[76.06,77.16],[77.16,77.38],[77.38,77.89],[77.89,78.25],[78.25,78.4],[78.4,78.54],[78.54,79.06],[79.06,79.64],[79.64,79.79],[79.79,79.93],[79.93,80.3],[80.67,81.47],[81.47,81.62],[81.62,82.49],[82.75,82.9],[82.9,83.26],[83.26,83.63],[83.63,84.43],[84.69,84.91],[84.91,85.12],[85.12,85.64],[85.64,85.78],[85.78,86.51],[86.51,86.81],[86.81,87.17]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Eugenicists testified in its favor.","start":88.07,"end":90.66,"words":[[88.12,88.91],[88.91,89.49],[89.49,89.62],[89.62,89.82],[89.82,90.21]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The quota system lasted until the Immigration and Nationality Act of 1965.","start":91.01,"end":97.17,"words":[[91.06,91.22],[91.22,91.67],[91.67,92.13],[92.13,92.59],[92.59,92.97],[92.97,93.13],[93.13,93.89],[93.89,94.12],[94.12,94.89],[94.89,95.12],[95.12,95.27],[95.27,96.65]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Mexican Americans: repatriation and “Operation Wetback”","start":98.07,"end":102.81,"words":[[98.13,98.82],[98.82,99.69],[99.99,101.12],[101.12,101.38],[101.38,102.16],[102.16,102.69]]},{"el":"s3","text":"During the Depression (1929–1936), federal, state and local officials pushed out hundreds of thousands of Mexicans and Mexican Americans; estimates range from about 400,000 to more than a million, and many were U.S. citizens, including children.","start":103.51,"end":122.23,"words":[[103.56,104.06],[104.06,104.2],[104.2,104.77],[104.77,107.26],[107.5,108.07],[108.32,108.68],[108.68,108.89],[108.89,109.32],[109.32,109.81],[109.81,110.1],[110.1,110.31],[110.31,110.88],[110.88,111.02],[111.02,111.59],[111.59,111.73],[111.73,112.37],[112.37,112.58],[112.58,113.15],[113.15,113.86],[114.11,114.68],[114.68,115.1],[115.1,115.39],[115.39,115.74],[115.74,116.88],[116.88,117.02],[117.02,117.16],[117.16,117.38],[117.38,117.52],[117.52,118.01],[118.26,118.48],[118.48,118.76],[118.76,118.9],[118.9,119.26],[119.61,120.25],[120.5,121.07],[121.07,121.71]]},{"el":"s3","text":"California apologized in 2005.","start":122.58,"end":125.92,"words":[[122.63,123.32],[123.32,124.17],[124.17,124.33],[124.33,125.41]]},{"el":"s3","text":"During World War II the Bracero program recruited Mexican workers; in 1954 “Operation Wetback” carried out mass deportations.","start":126.27,"end":135.42,"words":[[126.34,126.84],[126.84,127.13],[127.13,127.27],[127.27,127.84],[127.84,127.98],[127.98,128.62],[128.62,129.19],[129.19,129.76],[129.76,130.33],[130.33,130.76],[131.01,131.15],[131.15,132.15],[132.15,132.79],[132.79,133.22],[133.22,133.57],[133.57,133.79],[133.79,134.0],[134.0,134.86]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The government claimed over a million removals that year; historians consider that number inflated.","start":135.77,"end":142.08,"words":[[135.82,135.96],[135.96,136.59],[136.59,137.01],[137.01,137.29],[137.29,137.43],[137.43,137.86],[137.86,138.42],[138.42,138.63],[138.63,138.91],[139.15,139.85],[139.85,140.34],[140.34,140.56],[140.56,140.91],[140.91,141.61]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Japanese American incarceration","start":142.98,"end":145.3,"words":[[143.02,143.62],[143.62,144.23],[144.23,145.14]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Executive Order 9066 (February 1942) led to the forced removal of about 120,000 people of Japanese ancestry from the West Coast, two-thirds of them U.S. citizens, to camps behind barbed wire.","start":146.0,"end":162.74,"words":[[146.05,146.82],[146.82,147.05],[147.05,148.83],[148.83,149.45],[149.45,150.45],[150.72,150.95],[150.95,151.11],[151.11,151.26],[151.26,151.57],[151.57,152.11],[152.11,152.26],[152.26,152.65],[152.65,154.58],[154.58,154.97],[154.97,155.12],[155.12,155.74],[155.74,156.36],[156.36,156.66],[156.66,156.82],[156.82,157.13],[157.13,157.59],[157.86,158.32],[158.32,158.48],[158.48,158.71],[158.71,159.1],[159.48,160.18],[160.45,160.6],[160.6,160.99],[160.99,161.53],[161.53,161.84],[161.84,162.22]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The Supreme Court upheld it in Korematsu (1944).","start":163.09,"end":166.58,"words":[[163.13,163.27],[163.27,163.7],[163.7,163.91],[163.91,164.34],[164.34,164.48],[164.48,164.62],[164.62,165.12],[165.12,166.12]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The Civil Liberties Act of 1988 apologized and paid $20,000 to each surviving person who had been incarcerated, and in 2018 the Court said Korematsu “was gravely wrong the day it was decided.”","start":166.93,"end":179.93,"words":[[166.98,167.13],[167.13,167.48],[167.48,167.98],[167.98,168.19],[168.19,168.33],[168.33,169.4],[169.4,170.18],[170.18,170.39],[170.39,170.68],[170.68,171.88],[171.88,172.03],[172.03,172.24],[172.24,172.81],[172.81,173.16],[173.16,173.31],[173.31,173.52],[173.52,173.73],[173.73,174.66],[174.9,175.12],[175.12,175.26],[175.26,176.11],[176.11,176.25],[176.25,176.47],[176.47,176.68],[176.68,177.18],[177.18,177.39],[177.39,177.89],[177.89,178.1],[178.1,178.24],[178.24,178.46],[178.46,178.6],[178.6,178.81],[178.81,179.45]]},{"el":"h5","text":"Today","start":180.83,"end":181.58,"words":[[180.87,181.41]]},{"el":"s5","text":"In 2018 the “zero tolerance” policy separated thousands of children from their parents at the border.","start":182.28,"end":188.43,"words":[[182.33,182.46],[182.46,183.22],[183.22,183.34],[183.34,183.73],[183.73,184.23],[184.23,184.61],[184.61,185.25],[185.25,185.75],[185.75,185.88],[185.88,186.39],[186.39,186.64],[186.64,186.83],[186.83,187.34],[187.34,187.47],[187.47,187.59],[187.59,187.91]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Immigration detention holds tens of thousands of people on a given day.","start":188.78,"end":193.22,"words":[[188.83,189.51],[189.51,190.06],[190.06,190.47],[190.47,190.74],[190.74,190.88],[190.88,191.42],[191.42,191.56],[191.56,191.9],[191.9,192.03],[192.03,192.17],[192.17,192.51],[192.51,192.78]]},{"el":"s5","text":"In September 2025 the Supreme Court allowed immigration agents in the Los Angeles area to resume stops that a lower court had found relied on factors including apparent ethnicity, language and type of work, while the case continues.","start":193.57,"end":209.86,"words":[[193.62,193.78],[193.78,194.41],[194.41,195.67],[195.67,195.82],[195.82,196.3],[196.3,196.53],[196.53,196.93],[196.93,197.72],[197.72,198.35],[198.35,198.5],[198.5,198.66],[198.66,198.9],[198.9,199.53],[199.53,199.92],[199.92,200.08],[200.08,200.55],[200.55,200.95],[200.95,201.18],[201.18,201.34],[201.34,201.66],[201.66,201.89],[201.89,202.13],[202.13,202.52],[202.52,202.99],[202.99,203.15],[203.15,203.62],[203.62,204.26],[204.26,204.81],[204.81,205.52],[205.79,206.42],[206.42,206.66],[206.66,206.97],[206.97,207.13],[207.13,207.45],[207.72,208.04],[208.04,208.19],[208.19,208.51],[208.51,209.3]]},{"el":"s5","text":"A CAREN-style law protects anyone, citizen or not, from being reported to police in order to discriminate.","start":210.21,"end":217.22,"words":[[210.25,210.39],[210.39,211.1],[211.1,211.25],[211.25,211.82],[211.82,212.31],[212.56,213.06],[213.06,213.13],[213.13,213.42],[213.67,213.95],[213.95,214.31],[214.31,214.81],[214.81,214.95],[214.95,215.31],[215.31,215.45],[215.45,215.67],[215.67,215.81],[215.81,216.74]]}]}